A host's keyed packages evaluate each module once, not once an extension.

A keyed package's extensions already share one factory each across
folders, but jiti gives each extension's import a module cache of its
own, so a library two of its extensions import is evaluated once for
each. A keyed package's loads now share one module cache, the package's
own, so each file they reach is evaluated once for the package. Node's
module cache is neither read nor written, and every other package's loads
keep their modules their own. A reload, and a link moving off a copy, give
the package's next load a new cache. Its loads take turns, so none is
given a module another is still evaluating. A reload, or a link moving
off the copy, lets go of the loads waiting behind one that never ends;
those import as an unkeyed package's do. A load that runs past ten
seconds gives up its turn, and until it ends, the loads after it import
so too, each giving up its turn as it goes on; nothing kept is let go of,
and once it ends, it is kept and loads share again. A failed load forgets each module it was first to import, however
deep, and keeps those another extension was first to import as it ran.
A factory is kept only from a load through modules still its package's,
so none from a copy a link has moved off, or from modules let go of. An
import an extension makes as it runs takes no turn, as within one
extension today, so one that throws stays unfinished in the package's
cache until a reload.

